  "worked_solution": "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$",
